{"title":"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of the measures of all the face angles of a given complex polyhedral angle is equal to the sum of all its dihedral angles. Prove that the polyhedral angle is a trihedral angle.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> is certainly possible. For example, take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77ecf5115a0d8e8abb87e7a227981fb233054aae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle APB = \\angle APC = \\angle BPC = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"267\" height=\"15\" /> (so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/377291318df52e4f013ab2bf28ee82454a9e57da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PA$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/9/9f988caff51f7fc88ad40d1d25022c52ffd8f976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/e/30e049532148caaf1c48e37658b548a6255c8d59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> are mutually perpendicular). Then the three planes through P are also mutually perpendicular, so the two sums are both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/a/18ac5017c605c4cd861dc861b3f7ddc59410306c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$270^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>We show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> is not possible.\n</p><p>It is almost obvious that the sum of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> dihedral angles at P is less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9506d61661a7d046ee93b381ddb704684ffd15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. Take another plane which meets the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/377291318df52e4f013ab2bf28ee82454a9e57da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PA$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/9/9f988caff51f7fc88ad40d1d25022c52ffd8f976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/e/30e049532148caaf1c48e37658b548a6255c8d59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> etc at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/9/1090530ae883b627e090bfb5e450ef796febca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/7/4c71f19552a05ee5e2b31e94aff3fb986884bf31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B&#39;$\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b798193cb94562989018728e1151587c618655b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"16\" />, ... and so that the foot of the perpendicular from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to the plane lies inside the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-gon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/9/d6903c3ab9981561470474735b1f51308fb3f4df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"16\" /> then as we move <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> down the perpendicular the angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/e/c4e2cf86f457f826f89db8fe9d02d1a2f8d93a40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;PB&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> etc all increase. But when it reaches the plane their sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9506d61661a7d046ee93b381ddb704684ffd15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. Take any point O inside the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-gon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a6421529a15de4a7ffff18cb67062c5af351a7a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"72\" height=\"14\" /> . We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/4/9249c85b79570b180b978801604f295c715fa355.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle PBA + \\angle PBC &gt; \\angle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"215\" height=\"16\" />. Adding the n such equations we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/2/a22edb9cc6a20c056bfdd82d40739ee4d75ab228.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum(180^\\circ - APB) &gt; \\sum ABC = (n - 2) 180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"352\" height=\"31\" />. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f49b957d030c20cc7fe37bf661afec60ce45ad0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum APB... &lt; 360^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"147\" height=\"31\" />\nThe sum of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> angles between the planes is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/f/d9fddc58300352b82c304dcbba5a7433b14f492c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180(n-2)^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />. If we take a sphere center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, then the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/377291318df52e4f013ab2bf28ee82454a9e57da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PA$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/9/9f988caff51f7fc88ad40d1d25022c52ffd8f976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> intersect it at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/3/e739fac5f3a904b5d92e8827a77805bcc48ad167.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&quot;, B&quot;, ...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"18\" /> which form a spherical polygon. The angles of this polygon are the angles between the planes. We can divide the polygon into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/438ca8cfe4ef1f8af9be1b9ba6670a87cced8f11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> triangles. The angles in a spherical triangle sum to at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c4460b5100c3ce982ab5ebb20a8c3871e9cfdc54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. So the angles in the spherical polygon are at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/f/d9fddc58300352b82c304dcbba5a7433b14f492c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180(n-2)^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />. So we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/f/71f21940c927ea2324be81639415bdf48e05169e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n - 2) 180^\\circ &lt; 360^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"152\" height=\"20\" /> and hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46b15835991a65124d5def20e48b608d0269060e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &lt; 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO\" title=\"1981 USAMO\">1981 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1981\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1981_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1981 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017173702\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.030 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.049 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 227/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1267/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument siz